---
import "../styles/global.css";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Nav from "../components/Nav.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import PageHero from "../components/PageHero.astro";
import Section from "../components/Section.astro";
import Callout from "../components/Callout.astro";
import Prose from "../components/Prose.astro";
import LinkButton from "../components/LinkButton.astro";
import { REPO } from "../data/site";
import { docHref } from "../data/docs";

// Quoted with its source. It belongs here and not in the hero: it explains
// why the project exists, it isn't a claim about Orbit, and a statistic
// in a headline reads as a pitch.
const context = {
  headline: "Data centres used about 415 TWh in 2024, around 1.5% of global electricity.",
  growth: "Demand has grown about 12% a year since 2017.",
  source: "IEA Energy and AI",
  sourceHref: "https://www.iea.org/reports/energy-and-ai",
};

const beliefs = [
  {
    title: "Scaling by adding consumption is clumsy",
    body: "Adding machines, compute, or parameters just because it's possible is bad engineering. It hides the cost instead of paying it.",
  },
  {
    title: "Software is physical",
    body: "Digital doesn't mean clean. Every request costs energy, usually drawn from sources that aren't renewable.",
  },
  {
    title: "Comfort is philosophical",
    body: "If starting a server needs a tree of dependencies, it isn't elegant, even when it's fast. I wanted a toolchain I could read end to end.",
  },
  {
    title: "Doing more with less is respect",
    body: "For the machine, for the energy, and for the people who don't have exceptional hardware to work on.",
  },
];

const method = [
  {
    title: "Name the machine",
    body: "Processor, cores, memory, and whether the box is shared. Run to run noise is expected on a shared machine, so medians of repeats get reported.",
  },
  {
    title: "Bracket every pair",
    body: "Before and after runs alternate rather than measuring A fully and then B. A shared box drifts, and bracketing keeps the comparison honest.",
  },
  {
    title: "Report the failures",
    body: "Attempts that didn't pay off get published too, with the reason. A document that only lists wins isn't a measurement.",
  },
  {
    title: "Never fake energy",
    body: "CPU time is not joules. Without a hardware sensor, energy stays a proxy, labelled as one. No universal conversion factor gets applied.",
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="Foundation"
  description="Why Orbit exists: scaling by adding consumption is clumsy, software is physical, and efficiency has to be measured before it is claimed."
>
  <Nav />

  <main id="main" class="mx-auto w-full max-w-6xl px-6 sm:px-8">
    <PageHero
      eyebrow="Foundation"
      heading="How far less can go"
      lead="The hypothesis is simple to state and awkward to prove: a server shouldn't need so much energy to be fast. I'm testing it, and this page is what I'm testing."
    />

    <Section number="01" heading="Why this exists">
      <div class="grid gap-8 lg:grid-cols-[1.2fr_1fr] lg:gap-12">
        <Prose>
          <p>
            {context.headline} {context.growth} That growth is the reason a language that compiles
            fast and needs little at runtime is worth building at all.
          </p>
          <p>
            <a href={context.sourceHref} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">{context.source}</a>.
            It's context for the project, not a measurement of Orbit. I'm not claiming a causal
            link between the two.
          </p>
          <p>
            Orbit is not an ecological project that happens to make a language. It's an
            engineering project whose natural result is ecological. The engineering has to hold up
            first, or the rest is decoration.
          </p>
        </Prose>
        <Callout label="Source">
          <p class="text-lead text-balance font-normal">{context.headline}</p>
          <p class="text-small">{context.growth}</p>
          <p class="text-micro">
            {context.source}. A third party figure, quoted with its source rather than measured by
            me.
          </p>
        </Callout>
      </div>
    </Section>

    <Section number="02" heading="What I think is clumsy">
      <div class="grid gap-8 sm:grid-cols-2 sm:gap-x-12 sm:gap-y-12">
        {
          beliefs.map((belief) => (
            <div class="pt-8 sm:pt-12">
              <h3 class="text-lead text-balance">{belief.title}</h3>
              <p class="text-body mt-2 text-[var(--ink-2)]">{belief.body}</p>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>
      <Prose class="mt-10 sm:mt-12">
        <p>
          I have no interest in claiming Orbit beats a named language. Plenty of languages optimise
          for peak benchmark power, and that often needs more memory and energy to stay fast.
        </p>
      </Prose>
    </Section>

    <Section
      number="03"
      heading="How I measure"
      lead="The method is written down before the numbers, so I can't quietly change one to suit the other."
    >
      <div class="grid gap-8 lg:grid-cols-[1fr_1.05fr] lg:gap-12">
        <ol class="flex flex-col gap-6">
          {
            method.map((step, index) => (
              <li class="flex gap-4">
                <span class="font-mono text-small shrink-0 text-[var(--ink-3)]">
                  {String(index + 1).padStart(2, "0")}
                </span>
                <div>
                  <h3 class="text-small font-normal text-ink">{step.title}</h3>
                  <p class="text-small mt-1.5 leading-relaxed text-[var(--ink-2)]">{step.body}</p>
                </div>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ol>
        <div class="flex flex-col gap-4">
          <Callout variant="note" label="Where the method lives">
            <p>
              The proxies, and the rule that CPU time is never converted into joules, are in{" "}
              <a href={docHref("ENERGY.md")}>energy measurement</a>. What the runtime keeps is in{" "}
              <a href={docHref("PERF.md")}>performance</a>. The full run-by-run procedure is not
              written up as its own document yet.
            </p>
          </Callout>
          <Callout variant="measuring" label="What I don't know yet">
            <p>
              I don't have a clean measurement on a quiet machine yet. The component gains I can
              isolate don't show up end to end, because the round trip is dominated by the
              loopback, the client, and the per-request access log.
            </p>
            <p>
              I'd rather say that plainly than put a round number on the home page. When the
              numbers are clean, they'll be here, with the bad runs included.
            </p>
          </Callout>
        </div>
      </div>
    </Section>

    <Section number="04" heading="How it's built">
      <Prose>
        <p>
          The compiler is written in Orbit and compiles itself. That wasn't chosen for the logo.
          A toolchain you can't read is a toolchain you can't fix.
        </p>
        <p>
          Orbit compiles to C, and your platform's C compiler produces the executable. One file per
          service, no runtime to install beside it. That's what "needs little" has to mean in
          practice, or it means nothing.
        </p>
        <p>
          The project runs on one modest PC, with no team behind it. Every performance claim here
          has to survive that constraint, because it's the reason the project exists.
        </p>
      </Prose>
      <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
        <LinkButton href={docHref("architecture/SELF_HOSTING.md")} variant="secondary">
          How the bootstrap works
        </LinkButton>
        <LinkButton href={docHref("architecture/SOVEREIGNTY.md")} variant="tertiary">
          Trust and reproducibility
        </LinkButton>
        <LinkButton href={REPO.url} variant="tertiary">The repository</LinkButton>
      </div>
    </Section>

    <section class="mt-16 pt-16 sm:mt-24 sm:pt-24">
      <Prose>
        <h2>What I'm testing next</h2>
        <p>
          The honest next step isn't another transform. It's measuring the accept, parse, and send
          path on a machine I can account for, then deciding whether Superluminal is worth
          continuing at all. If the answer is no, I'll say so here and in the repository.
        </p>
        <p>
          To follow that, the <a href={docHref("ROADMAP.md")}>roadmap</a> and{" "}
          <a href={docHref("STATUS.md")}>dated status</a> pages are the two places I update
          honestly, including the parts that didn't work.
        </p>
      </Prose>
    </section>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</Base>
